TASK:{"title":"SWEN3204 Software Architecture – Assignment 2: Short-Answer Questions","overview":"Answer six short questions applying architecture concepts to the CampusEats scenario. The assignment is worth 40 marks, including 4 marks for communication. Submit an individual, single-document response."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Q1 Quality attributes","weight":null,"max":6,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"Two attributes that clearly matter most are chosen and justified from the scenario. Both scenarios have all six parts and both response measures are measurable."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"Suitable attributes are chosen with some justification. Scenarios are mostly complete but one is missing a part or has a response measure that is not measurable."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"Attributes are named with little or no justification, or the scenarios are not in six-part form or are not measurable."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or irrelevant."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Q2 Architectural style","weight":null,"max":6,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"A clear recommendation justified by at least two trade-offs that are specific to the scenario (team size, load profile, known problems). The downside of the recommendation is acknowledged."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"A clear recommendation with two trade-offs, but one is generic or not linked to the scenario, or no downside is acknowledged."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"A recommendation with only one trade-off, or with generic textbook advantages and disadvantages only."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or no recommendation."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Q3 Availability calculation","weight":null,"max":8,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":7,"max":8,"desc":"Parts (a), (b) and (c) are all correct with working shown, to the requested precision."},{"label":"Proficient","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"The method is correct throughout but there is one arithmetic or rounding slip, or exactly one part is incorrect."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":4,"desc":"The series calculation is attempted but two or more parts are incorrect, or working is not shown."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or no correct element."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Q4 Resilience pattern","weight":null,"max":5,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":5,"desc":"An appropriate pattern is named, its mechanism is explained correctly, it is applied to the outage, and at least one drawback or tuning consideration is given."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"An appropriate pattern is named and explained correctly, but it is only loosely applied to the outage or no drawback or tuning consideration is given."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"A pattern is named but the explanation is incorrect or very thin."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or an unsuitable pattern."}]},{"id":"C5","name":"Q5 Consistency and availability","weight":null,"max":6,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"Both choices are justified, the CAP theorem is used correctly, and a suitable concrete technique is named for each case."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"Both choices are reasonable and techniques are named, but the CAP theorem is explained incorrectly or imprecisely, or one technique is missing."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"Choices are stated with little justification, or the CAP theorem is not used."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted."}]},{"id":"C6","name":"Q6 Architecture decision record","weight":null,"max":5,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":5,"desc":"Context, Decision and Consequences are all present and specific to the scenario, with at least one positive and one negative consequence."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"All three sections are present but the consequences are one-sided (no negative consequence) or the context is generic."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"One or more sections are missing or the record does not describe a decision."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted."}]},{"id":"C7","name":"Communication and format","weight":null,"max":4,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"Every answer is within 150 words, terminology is precise, the required headings are used and sources are cited in IEEE style."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":3,"desc":"Clear writing, but one answer exceeds the word limit or there are minor citation or formatting problems."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"Several answers exceed the word limit, or the writing is hard to follow, or sources are not cited."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Format requirements ignored."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"partial","where":"Question 1"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Question 2"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Question 3"},{"id":"R4","met":"partial","where":"Question 4"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Question 5"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Question 6"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"References"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Question 3 (c)","issue":"End-to-end availability incorrectly reported as 99.9975%; the paired payment availability (0.999975) must be multiplied by the other components: 0.99800125 × 0.999975 ≈ 99.80%, giving ≈1.5 h downtime, not 0.018 h."},{"where":"Question 3 (c)","issue":"Downtime of 0.018 hours is not given to one decimal place as required."},{"where":"Question 5","issue":"CAP theorem misapplied: partition tolerance cannot be sacrificed; during a partition the choice is between consistency and availability."},{"where":"Question 1","issue":"Availability response measure ('stay up most of the time') is not measurable."},{"where":"Question 6","issue":"No negative consequence stated in the ADR."},{"where":"Whole document","issue":"No assumptions explicitly stated, contrary to the task instruction."}],"overall":"A strong, well-written submission with scenario-specific justifications throughout. The main weaknesses are the conceptual error in Q3(c) end-to-end availability, an imprecise CAP explanation in Q5, a one-sided ADR, a non-measurable availability response measure, and a word-limit breach in Q2.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Proficient","mark":4.0,"scale":6.0,"points":4.0,"max":6.0,"reasoning":"Performance and availability are well justified from the scenario (900 lunch orders, 4.2 s p95, payment outage). Performance scenario is complete and measurable, but the availability response measure ('stay up most of the time') is not measurable, so Proficient.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 1","quote":"Response measure: the system should stay up most of the time."},{"where":"Question 1","quote":"the p95 response time is under 1.5 s and no requests are dropped"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Excellent","mark":5.0,"scale":6.0,"points":5.0,"max":6.0,"reasoning":"Clear recommendation (modular monolith) with scenario-specific trade-offs (five-developer team, single-VM lunchtime load, 20-minute deployment downtime) and the downside explicitly acknowledged. The second argument is more justification than trade-off, so not the top of the range.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 2","quote":"With five developers, running eight or ten services would mean more time spent on infrastructure than on features."},{"where":"Question 2","quote":"The trade-off I accept is that modules cannot be scaled independently"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Proficient","mark":5.0,"scale":8.0,"points":5.0,"max":8.0,"reasoning":"Parts (a) and (b) are correct with working (0.99301, 5.0 h). Part (c) is conceptually wrong: the student reports only the paired payment availability (99.9975%) and omits recombination with the other three components; correct end-to-end is ≈99.80% and ≈1.5 h downtime. Exactly one part incorrect, so Proficient.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 3","quote":"So the new availability of placing an order is 99.9975%, which reduces the downtime to 0.000025 × 720 = 0.018 hours"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Proficient","mark":4.0,"scale":5.0,"points":4.0,"max":5.0,"reasoning":"Circuit Breaker is correctly named and its states/mechanism explained well, and it is concretely applied to the 25-minute outage. However, no explicit drawback or tuning consideration is stated as such; the threshold example is embedded in the mechanism, not offered as a consideration.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 4","quote":"Students would have seen \"payment temporarily unavailable, please try again or pay at the counter\" instead of the whole app hanging for 25 minutes."}]},{"id":"C5","level":"Proficient","mark":4.0,"scale":6.0,"points":4.0,"max":6.0,"reasoning":"Both choices (availability for browsing, consistency for payment) are reasonable and justified, with concrete techniques (Redis TTL cache, idempotency keys). But CAP is used imprecisely: partition tolerance cannot be 'given up'; during a partition one must choose between C and A.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 5","quote":"so for payments we choose consistency and availability and give up partition tolerance"}]},{"id":"C6","level":"Proficient","mark":4.0,"scale":5.0,"points":4.0,"max":5.0,"reasoning":"Context, Decision and Consequences are all present and specific to CampusEats (synchronous tablet calls, 4.2 s p95, offline tablets). However, the consequences are one-sided: only benefits are listed, with no negative consequence such as eventual notification delay or added operational complexity.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 6","quote":"Placing an order no longer waits for the outlet, so the response time improves."}]},{"id":"C7","level":"Proficient","mark":3.0,"scale":4.0,"points":3.0,"max":4.0,"reasoning":"Writing is clear, headings are used in order, and IEEE-style citations [1], [2] with a reference list are present. However, Question 2 clearly exceeds the 150-word limit (roughly 190 words), and Question 4 is borderline, so Proficient.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 2","quote":"A rewrite would freeze feature work for months without directly fixing either problem."}]}]}
